+## Confirmed findings
+- [Should-fix] §4.9 Observation bullet; Key abstractions observe::classify — Excluding only the node manager's seat and clones from cascade grouping does not stop a workspace closure from retiring the node manager's teamspace, and reconciliation does not reopen a seat whose teamspace is Retired; the "reopened" guarantee fails for workspace closure.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/observe/classify.rs:264-289 — the ws_gone loop does `retire.push(ts.to_any())` for the teamspace before any per-seat grouping, so the seat exemption does not reach it. src/reconcile/desired.rs:137-147 builds DesiredTabs only when both teamspace and seat are Active, so a Retired teamspace yields no tab to recreate. The spec (§4.9, line ~320) exempts only "the node manager's seat and clones" and then promises "reconciliation recreates the workspace if needed".
+  fix-shape hint: Add one rule to §4.9: the teamspace holding the node manager is also excluded from workspace-cascade retirement (dropped from the `retire` list), and state what happens to that teamspace's other seats.
+- [Should-fix] §4.9 'An observed move of its pane or tab (outside a plan) is not adopted as its placement; reconciliation restores the recorded tab.' — The existing observer adopts/flags out-of-plan moves as moved_out and the planner skips them; no move-back primitive exists, so "restores the recorded tab" has no mechanism.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/observe/step.rs:237-250 emits move_request and sets moved_out; src/observe/tests.rs:1094-1105 asserts a user-moved clone is "rebound into the known tab"; src/observe/tests.rs:1111-1160 and src/reconcile/tests.rs:880 assert a moved_out seat gets no CreateTab; src/reconcile/herdr_exec.rs:151 filters on `!t.moved_out`. grep finds no pane/tab move operation in the Herdr client or executor — only the fake's `user_move_pane` test hook (src/herdr/fake.rs:411). Bead hg-k4l.9 hedges "if the observe layer never adopts placement, the test documents that".
+  fix-shape hint: Either drop "reconciliation restores the recorded tab" and specify that the move is tolerated and surfaced (moved_out stays, warning), or name a concrete move-back effect and the observer change that suppresses moved_out for the node manager.
+- [Should-fix] §4.4 / Key abstractions cli::daemon_error "anything else → the error, exit 1" — A daemon that is reachable but hung, slow, or returning a malformed frame surfaces as ClientError::Frame, not Unavailable, so it falls to exit 1 ("do not guess") instead of the exit-5 "carry on, retry later" branch.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/daemon/client.rs — ClientError has a separate `Frame(#[from] FrameError)` variant; Client::call propagates write_frame/read_frame failures with `?`, so a read/write timeout on a connected socket, a truncated frame or an undecodable frame is Frame. Only connect/ensure failures, request-id mismatch and herdr-socket mismatch become Unavailable. §4.1 itself admits a stale pairing "can produce a frame error". The hg-k4l.2 test list covers only "no reachable daemon".
+  fix-shape hint: Map ClientError::Frame (I/O, timeout, decode) to the exit-5 path in cli::daemon_error, or state explicitly in §4.4 that it is exit 1 and why; add a test for a hung daemon.
+- [Should-fix] §4.4 / cli::daemon_error closed mapping — Remote error codes other than Integrity, notably the daemon-side `Remote{code: Unavailable}` ("still starting; retry shortly" / "failed to start"), are not classified and fall to exit 1, losing the retry-later guidance for the commonest transient case at SessionStart.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/daemon/server.rs:213-222 returns IpcErrorCode::Unavailable for "daemon failed to start: {why}" and "daemon is still starting (first pass); retry shortly"; src/daemon/registry.rs:50 also builds one. src/daemon/client.rs:69 passes IpcResult::Error through as ClientError::Remote unchanged, distinct from ClientError::Unavailable (client.rs:62, 103). §4.4 maps only ClientError::Unavailable, Remote{Integrity} and NoInstance. The §4.5 exit-1 branch never says to run /seat later. The VersionMismatch sub-claim is weaker (§4.1 does not bump IPC_VERSION).
+  fix-shape hint: Add `Remote{code: Unavailable}` to the exit-5 arm of cli::daemon_error (same generic retry text); decide and state whether VersionMismatch joins it.
+- [Nit] §4.3 integrity suffix; §4.5 step 6; §4.6 'if S is the node manager → None' — When the node manager's own seat fails an integrity check, the suffix tells it to report to itself; the spec never checks whether the failing caller is the node manager, and the node-manager duties arrive only in a successful seat.resolve reply.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: §4.3 builds the suffix from graph.toml's node_manager_seat "independent of the broken object"; §4.12 uses the "report it to the user" form only when node_manager_seat does not resolve to a live seat. No caller-aware case exists (searched for node manager / own seat / itself / subject). Refute seat (REJECT) notes §4.5 step 6 already says "(or the user)", which bounds the impact; the two CONFIRMs concede the same, hence Nit.
+  fix-shape hint: One sentence in §4.3/§4.12: when the failing caller is the node manager seat, use the "report it to the user" suffix form.
+- [Nit] §4.1 'A stale daemon paired with a new CLI can at worst produce a frame error instead of a message' — The version-skew rationale names the wrong pairing: only the daemon emits IpcErrorCode, so a stale daemon never sends `integrity`; the frame-error hazard is a new daemon with an old CLI, which the spec neither analyses nor mitigates.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/ipc.rs:33-44 — IpcErrorCode is a plain snake_case enum with no `#[serde(other)]` catch-all, so an old binary fails to decode "integrity"; src/daemon/client.rs:29 routes FrameError to the "anything else" arm (exit 1, not 6). A new CLI on a stale daemon just gets `Internal` and exit 1 with no frame error. Refute seat (REJECT) agrees the label is loose and the no-bump decision still holds either way.
+  fix-shape hint: Reword §4.1 to name new-daemon + old-CLI as the degraded pair, state the accepted degradation, and keep the no-bump decision.
+- [Nit] §4.12 'WhoReply gains manager_chain: Vec<{seat, name}>'; §4.1 'without bumping IPC_VERSION' — New Vec-typed reply fields (manager_chain, reports) have no serde(default) requirement, so a new CLI reading a still-running old daemon's reply fails to parse `who` and `seat` until the daemon restarts. [lanes: premortem]
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/threads/mod.rs:253 — WhoReply is a plain derived Deserialize struct with no per-field defaults; a missing Vec field is a serde "missing field" error while a missing Option is None. §4.6 (spec line 180) requires serde(default) only on stored record fields; §4.12 (lines 267-270) says nothing for reply fields. §4.13 removes who's daemon-free fallback, so the parse failure would hit the §4.4 "anything else" arm (exit 1). Window is bounded to upgrade-until-daemon-restart.
+  fix-shape hint: Add one line to §4.12: new reply fields (manager_chain, reports, manager, node_manager) carry `#[serde(default)]`.
+- [Nit] §4.7 'Target must exist and be live'; §4.8 first-seat assignment; §4.9 reopen 'for an active seat' — Neither node-manager set ("live" = not retired) nor first-seat assignment requires the target to be Active with a placement, yet reconcile only reopens active seats, so a dormant node manager is never reopened.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: §4.6 treats a pointer to a retired seat as transparent, so "live" means not retired; §4.8 itself writes "a single active member `main`" for the office template, showing template members can be non-active; §4.9 refuses effects that deactivate the node manager but never checks state at designation time, and plan_effects creates tabs/panes only "for an active seat". Refute seat (REJECT) notes the shipped office path is safe; the CONFIRMs agree, hence Nit.
+  fix-shape hint: Require the target to be Active at `node-manager set` and have the first-seat rule pick the first Active member (or auto-activate on assign).
+- [Nit] §4.11 'inside do_retire_seat ... compute the retiring seat's effective manager M'; Key abstractions model::manager Errors — The nearest-surviving-manager walk can return StoreError::Integrity, and on the observed-cascade route (no plan to refuse) the spec does not say whether the retirement aborts, skips reparenting, or falls back to the node manager. [lanes: failure-mode]
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: model::manager "returns StoreError::Integrity for a cycle or an unresolvable pointer"; do_retire_seat is shared by all seven routes including CascadeMutation::retire_seat; §4.11 says observed cascades reparent "without a preview". Integrity handling is specified only for seat.resolve/who/show (§4.2, §4.3, §4.12). Reproduce seat notes spec lines 239-240 reject a design where "the observed path would error instead of reopening", which this gap recreates. Trigger requires out-of-band graph corruption (§4.6).
+  demoted: profile states a single-operator local tool with a Git-backed, hand-repairable graph, and the trigger is hand-edited corruption only, so an unspecified error path on an observed cascade is a Nit here (reproduce and ground seats split Should-fix/Nit).
+  fix-shape hint: One sentence in §4.11 choosing the disposition for an integrity error during observed-cascade reparenting (abort atomically and surface, or fall back to the live node manager with a warning).
+- [Nit] §4.4 'Not being inside a Herdr pane (HERDR_SOCKET_PATH unset) is not a daemon outage: it gets its own variant ... never the retry-later text' — The existing client raises "not inside Herdr" only for CallMode::Ensure; NoEnsure reads (who, ops, undo, status) outside Herdr with no daemon running return Unavailable("not running") and would get exit 5 plus retry text, contradicting the "never" promise.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/daemon/client.rs:124-126 — `if mode == CallMode::Ensure && env.herdr_socket.is_none()` is the only "not inside Herdr" check; who.rs:42, status.rs:62, undo.rs:42 and ops.rs:95 use NoEnsure. The spec does not say which CallModes raise the new variant. Only shows when no daemon is running (a running daemon answers NoEnsure reads outside Herdr).
+  fix-shape hint: State in §4.4 that the new variant is raised for both call modes (or check HERDR_SOCKET_PATH in cli::daemon_error itself), or explicitly exempt NoEnsure reads.
