+## Rejected (with reason)
+- §4.4 generic exit-5 line "ask your manager or coordinator"; §4.12; §4.13 — exit-5 text names an unreachable manager. 3× REJECT. B2 asks only for "possibly asking its coordinator"; "coordinator" is a Herdr-level relation known without the graph, the seat text also says "Tell the user", and the user rejected daemon-down fallbacks (B1). Wording nit at most.
+- §4.9 Observation bullet — node manager reopened with no backoff/attempt cap. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Nit. B11 ("gets reopened if it ever is closed") is the user's explicit requirement; CURRENT-DESIGN.md line 23 says agent exit in a retained pane ends occupancy only and does not trigger reopen; `node-manager set` is the named remedy. Ground seat dissent (planner.rs:467,777,991 `attempts: 0` on each fresh effect, so backoff does not accumulate across reopen cycles) is concrete but does not contradict a stated requirement; noted as a possible follow-up (attention state for repeated closure).
+- §4.12 reports; §4.2 'no partial paths'; hg-k4l.11/16 — manager-pointer integrity fails the whole seat.resolve. 3× REJECT. B4 explicitly rejected partial-context degradation ("lgtm" x3); cycles/dangling pointers arise only from out-of-band edits (§4.6); fail-loud is the chosen policy. Whether `reports` computation tolerates other seats' bad pointers is an implementation-time choice.
+- §4.10 warning text; §3 — imperative `plan node-manager set <seat>` to every agent. 3× REJECT. B12 asked for exactly this per-command warning; B13 makes a second `set` a sanctioned move; a wrong pick is recoverable by another `set`. Wording suggestion only.
+- §4.4 'Every command' vs five named; 2/5/6/else-1 mapping — scope contradiction and exit-code collision. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Nit. Existing codes are 2, 3 (EXIT_STILL_RUNNING, src/daemon/client.rs:15-17) and 4 (EXIT_ALREADY_APPLIED, src/cli/plan.rs:13); nothing uses 5 or 6. "Every" widens the five, not contradicts it. Command-specific Remote arms (src/cli/request.rs:137-142) are handled before the fallthrough; ground seat's suggestion to say so is a one-sentence clarification.
+- §4.4 'run /seat again later'; §4.5 — no hook re-runs /seat after recovery. 3× REJECT. B3: "all sessions just carry on doing what they can and hope graph recovers"; coordinator recovery is "outside our work". Accepted tradeoff.
+- §4.11 reparent target M on an unassigned graph — M may be None, spec silent. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Nit. effective_manager returns Option; "rewrite to M" with M=None clears the field, which is behaviourally identical to the §4.6 fallback; retired pointers are transparent so no reading produces an integrity error. One-line clarification at most.
+- §4.8 'unset' vs §4.6/§4.10 'missing or retired' — first-seat auto-assign on a dangling pointer with no live seats. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Nit. State arises only from out-of-band edits; §4.8 explicitly accepts no auto-assign on already-seated graphs; `node-manager set` handles the dangling case (§4.7). Blob precondition is a deliberate tradeoff. One-phrase wording fix at most.
+- §4.11 undo of double-rewritten pointer needs reverse order. 3× REJECT. Premise fails: §4.11 computes M "skipping seats being retired in the same mutation", so each pointer is rewritten at most once per mutation and order does not matter.
+- §4.7 ManagerEdit relied_on vs concurrent cycle. 3× REJECT. §4.9 line 238 puts the cycle guard in make_plan; plan::managers (line 303) says make_plan "already covers the replan in plan/apply.rs"; src/plan/apply.rs ~130-139 recomputes make_plan on the committed base. The second apply's replan refuses the cycle.
+- §4.9 'existing reconcile planner reopens it' — reconcile may not react to observed closure. 3× REJECT. src/observe/step.rs:597 calls reconciler.step after every observation step; src/reconcile/mod.rs:40-41 has a 60s periodic tick; src/reconcile/planner.rs:532 already emits CreateWorkspace. B11 bounds no latency.
+- Key abstractions plan::managers Errors; CommandError classifier — plan-path integrity surfaces as exit 1. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Nit. B4/Goal scope exit 6 to /seat, who and show; plan commands are run by operators, not seat agents, and the StoreError message still names holder/field/target. Ground seat's src/plan/commands.rs:36-39 citation confirms the mechanism, which the majority already concedes; no stated requirement is contradicted.
+- §4.9 cycle refusal; §4.7 ManagerEdit validation — guards block repair of pre-existing corruption. 3× REJECT. §4.7 validates the post-edit chain, so `manager clear` on a seat in a cycle passes; §4.7 handles a dangling node_manager_seat explicitly; the §4.9 guard is scoped to seats whose effective manager changes. Speculative.
+- §4.9 placement-freeze, deactivation and TeamspaceRetire guards overreach B11. 3× REJECT. The user's B11 quote is "cant be moved either. same tab, stays open, gets reopened"; B8 says "can't be retired". Only effects that retire, relocate or deactivate that one seat are refused; `node-manager set` is the named escape.
+- §4.9 guards; §4.7 — no supported teardown of the node manager. 3× REJECT. Permanence is B8/B11 by explicit user choice; the role-move remedy is specified; node decommissioning is out of scope.
+- §4.6 'TeamspaceRecord.default_manager' — second pointer source nobody asked for (YAGNI). 2× CONFIRM Should-fix, 1× REJECT. OVERRULED to rejected on the ground seat's evidence, verified by this gate: run.md line 9 states the hg-au7 scope as "manager seats: seat/teamspace manager pointer", and coverage-round-1-requirements.md line 6 records R6 (B5) as "a seat can point to a manager seat, directly or through its teamspace's default". Both CONFIRMs rest on the premise that no requirement asks for a teamspace default; the settled scope does. Dropping the field would cut scope, not trim gold-plating. The complexity cost the CONFIRMs enumerate is real and is noted for the record; challenging it is a scope question for the human, not a spec gap.
+- §4.6 transparent retired pointers vs §4.11 explicit reparenting — redundant, and resurrection is lossy. 3× REJECT. B6 requires the explicit rewrite with visible previews; the transparent rule is the stated defensive fallback for resurrected seats and stale pointers (§4.6 says so); no requirement says resurrection restores old reports.
+- §4.8 first-seat auto-assign makes a scratch seat the permanent node manager. 3× REJECT. B10/S1 ("the first seat created") is the user's explicit reading; §4.8 says the assignment "is a listed plan effect", so the preview shows it; `node-manager set` moves the role.
+- §4.12 manager/node_manager/reports/threads_seat are a /seat-time snapshot. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Nit. The user left the communication channel open ("how they communicate is open") and scoped sessions as best-effort; who/show always read live; re-running /seat refreshes. Ground seat's suggestion (skill line: re-run `seat` before escalating) is a cheap documentation addition.
+- §4.10 unassigned warning reads graph.toml + seat scan on every command. 3× REJECT. B12 asks for a per-command warning; §4.10 states "any read failure prints nothing" and "for a warning only; it is not a daemon fallback"; HEAD is a commit snapshot. Ground seat notes seats live in `seats/<slug>/seat.toml` (src/model/seat.rs:1), so §4.10 should say it lists seats, not only reads graph.toml — a wording nit.
+- §4.4 'Every command that calls the daemon routes its client errors through this helper' vs hook-path commands. 1× CONFIRM Should-fix, 2× REJECT. Reproduce seat cites src/bootstrap/setup_claude.rs:170-172 (hook runs `session-report --from-hook claude`) and src/cli/session.rs:3-7 (must never fail the hook, spools on daemon error). Refute seat addresses the same files: §3 ("every daemon-calling command touched here") and §4.13's enumerated conversion list do not include session-report, which keeps its own spool. Ground seat's claim that no other hook-path command exists is contradicted by both other seats' citations, but the refute seat's scoping answers the dissent. Default stands; a one-line note in §4.13 naming session-report as exempt would remove the ambiguity.
+- §4.2 silent skips made fatal without establishing plan-reachability of dangling references. 3× REJECT. B4 approved fatal integrity errors ("lgtm" x3) and rejected partial context; template/application retire semantics are existing code outside this spec. Suggested follow-up bead: audit whether TemplateEdit withdrawal and ApplicationRetire can strand a template_ref.
+- §4.9 make_plan guard derives post-plan state from body.effects alone. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Should-fix. PlanEffect (src/plan/types.rs:13) has stable `kind` strings and a `detail` JSON that existing code already branches on (src/undo/preview.rs); the guard also receives the pre-plan `tree`. The ground seat's concern (no stated overlay or normalized detail) is addressed by the refute seat's reading of the same type; at most a note saying which effect fields the guard reads.
